Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Exercise 12.7(g)", "Section",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.96310705517325*^9, 
  3.963107062613443*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"511630e4-23d0-416c-8a4b-\
6426ffab7e61"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
Show that the percentage change of 1 + cos^2(\[Iota]) in this range is about \
7%. This will be the uncertainty in the inferred distance for a face-on \
signal, even though the amplitudes themselves are measured to 1% accuracy.\
\>", "Subsubsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.963107188461945*^9},
 TextJustification->1.,ExpressionUUID->"082b3203-f6a8-4448-82c1-29eb0b4117a5"],

Cell["\<\
This part investigates how the uncertainty in the inclination angle (found in \
part f) affects the inferred amplitude of the gravitational wave, and \
therefore the inferred distance.

From Chapter 9, Schutz Eq. (9.100), the amplitude of the h+ polarization is \
proportional to the factor (1 + Cos[\[Iota]]^2). We need to calculate how \
much this factor changes as inclination varies from iota = 0 (perfectly \
face-on) to the maximum value of iota = 0.531829 radians that we found in \
Exer. 12.7(f).\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.9631070753483877`*^9, {3.963107140946602*^9, 3.963107155741613*^9}, {
   3.963107187052649*^9, 3.963107345194765*^9}, {3.963107420721807*^9, 
   3.963107437962178*^9}, {3.9631075141243668`*^9, 3.963107527319075*^9}, 
   3.963107569351553*^9, 3.9631076106924353`*^9, {3.963108898709683*^9, 
   3.963108904309156*^9}},
 TextJustification->1.,ExpressionUUID->"6bb79c57-101e-4bfd-a07e-4a171d0553cb"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"\n", 
  RowBox[{"(*", " ", 
   RowBox[{
   "Define", " ", "a", " ", "function", " ", "for", " ", "the", " ", 
    "amplitude", " ", "factor"}], " ", "*)"}], "\n", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"amplitudeFactor", "[", "\[Iota]_", "]"}], " ", ":=", " ", 
   RowBox[{"1", " ", "+", " ", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"Cos", "[", "\[Iota]", "]"}], "^", "2"}]}]}]}]], "Code",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.9631074244969482`*^9},
 CellLabel->"In[85]:=",ExpressionUUID->"825214a1-37d1-464e-a53a-758e0d08191d"],

Cell["\<\
Now we apply this function at the two extreme values of inclination:\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.963107444814027*^9, 
  3.9631074763148727`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"25465717-2684-4015-8c9b-\
7a35b33237bb"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"\n", 
  RowBox[{"(*", " ", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{
    "Calculate", " ", "the", " ", "factor", " ", "for", " ", "a", " ", 
     "perfectly", " ", "face"}], "-", 
    RowBox[{"on", " ", "system", " ", 
     RowBox[{"(", 
      RowBox[{"iota", " ", "=", " ", "0"}], ")"}]}]}], " ", "*)"}], "\n", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"factorFaceOn", " ", "=", " ", 
    RowBox[{"amplitudeFactor", "[", "0", "]"}]}], "\n", "\n", 
   RowBox[{"(*", " ", 
    RowBox[{
    "Calculate", " ", "the", " ", "factor", " ", "at", " ", "the", " ", 
     "maximum", " ", "possible", " ", "inclination", " ", "from", " ", "part",
      " ", 
     RowBox[{"(", "f", ")"}]}], " ", "*)"}], "\n", 
   RowBox[{"factorMaxIota", " ", "=", " ", 
    RowBox[{"amplitudeFactor", "[", "0.5318295896944989", "]"}]}], "\n", 
   RowBox[{"(*", " ", 
    RowBox[{
    "Calculate", " ", "the", " ", "factor", " ", "at", " ", "the", " ", 
     "midway", " ", "inclination", " ", "from", " ", "part", " ", 
     RowBox[{"(", "f", ")"}]}], " ", "*)"}], "\n", 
   RowBox[{"factorMidwayIota", " ", "=", " ", 
    RowBox[{"amplitudeFactor", "[", 
     RowBox[{"0.5318295896944989", "/", "2"}], "]"}]}]}]}]], "Code",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.963107480990264*^9, {3.963107703185472*^9, 3.963107705104083*^9}, {
   3.963109136967292*^9, 3.963109171529545*^9}},
 CellLabel->"In[95]:=",ExpressionUUID->"dfa2e333-b08d-4df1-b0e9-a4387e38fdec"],

Cell["\<\
Now we calculate the percentage change between these two values. The \
percentage change is the absolute difference divided by the mid-point value \
(midway between face-on and maximum likely inclination), multiplied by 100 to \
convert to percent:\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.963107535117148*^9, 3.963107550857079*^9}, {
  3.9631090345148897`*^9, 3.963109104997694*^9}},
 TextJustification->1.,ExpressionUUID->"bf544995-19c7-44cf-925e-f3f916fe7e0e"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"percentageChange", " ", "=", " ", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"Abs", "[", 
     RowBox[{"factorMaxIota", " ", "-", " ", "factorFaceOn"}], "]"}], " ", "/",
     " ", "factorMidwayIota"}], " ", "*", " ", "100"}]}]], "Code",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.96310757610375*^9, 3.963109110771151*^9, {3.963109186445389*^9, 
   3.963109190799529*^9}},
 CellLabel->"In[98]:=",ExpressionUUID->"a3f59929-434a-4bc4-90e2-8c93cc1222ef"],

Cell["\<\
The result is approximately 13.3%, which is almost twice the value of \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]about 7%\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] stated in the \
problem. A likely explanation is that the 7% uncertainty is about the midway \
value, so we can justify dividing by two:



\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.963108964151681*^9, {3.9631092194918737`*^9, 3.9631092969489403`*^9}, 
   3.963109370217888*^9},
 TextJustification->1.,ExpressionUUID->"f10fcbe1-9041-412a-aba3-a0615e794743"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"percentageChange", "/", "2"}]], "Code",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.963109345659561*^9, 3.9631093479329147`*^9}},
 CellLabel->"In[99]:=",ExpressionUUID->"70d2d061-fbef-4bac-a495-1a1158a065e5"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Rounding off, we confirm an uncertainty of about 7% about the midway \
inclination.\n\n",
 StyleBox["Discussion ",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 "\n\nThis pair of exercises provides a crucial insight into the challenges \
of gravitational wave astronomy. As discussed in Section 12.3 of the \
textbook, the inclination angle is a key parameter needed to accurately \
determine the distance to a standard siren.\n\nOur calculations show that for \
nearly face-on systems (which produce the strongest and most easily \
detectable signals), even a small 1% uncertainty in the measured polarization \
amplitudes leads to a very large uncertainty in the inclination angle (up to \
30\[Degree]).\n\nFurthermore, this large uncertainty in iota propagates into \
a significant uncertainty (about 7%) in the inferred amplitude of the wave. \
Since the inferred distance is inversely proportional to the measured \
amplitude, this translates directly into a ~7% uncertainty in the distance \
measurement. This makes it difficult to get a precise measurement of \
cosmological parameters like the Hubble-Lema\[CapitalIota]tre constant from a \
single, nearly face-on event."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.963109375060801*^9, 3.963109478379249*^9}, {
  3.963109518239765*^9, 3.963109518455861*^9}, {3.963109561113907*^9, 
  3.963109564671702*^9}, {3.963116486685445*^9, 3.96311652211084*^9}, {
  3.963117480367655*^9, 3.963117480532707*^9}},
 TextJustification->1.,ExpressionUUID->"8bfff26b-4c39-407f-a293-04667f583c11"]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Exercise 12.9 ", "Section",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.963118858759201*^9, 3.9631188810518513`*^9}, {
  3.9631189194197607`*^9, 3.963118948159627*^9}},
 TextJustification->1.,ExpressionUUID->"61201817-ce55-4e19-adb1-078f6a3d43a8"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
Here we examine how an optimum all-sky search might correct for signal \
modulation and pulsar spin-down, and we use that to do a rough estimation of \
the number of arithmetic operations that would be required to search for a \
gravitational wave pulsar in six months of data. Each arithmetic operation is \
called, in the language of computing, a floating-point operation. For the \
purposes of this calculation we assume that the search goes up to 2 kHz in \
gravitational wave frequency, because theoretically it would be possible for \
a neutron star to spin at 1 kHz. We assume that the data are sampled at 4 \
kHz, which is the minimum sampling rate to allow a 2 kHz bandwidth search. \
The method will be to demodulate the recorded data for a particular patch on \
the sky, as described in the text, then to compensate for a particular \
spin-down value by the method described below, and then to do a Fourier \
transform for each combination of sky patch and spin-down value, in order to \
find a signal. Since this is a rough estimate, we regularly round our results \
to one significant figure. It should be emphasized that there can be other \
approaches to this problem that are more efficient, but not by huge factors. \
The approach here is straightforward to describe and shows rather readily \
that an optimum search is far out of reach.\
\>", "Subsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9631190040905743`*^9, 
  3.963119012694788*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"b669ab60-eefb-469b-8658-\
19dc7d41639d"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[TextData[{
 "(a) Show that if the duration of the observation is ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   TemplateBox[<|"boxes" -> FormBox[
       RowBox[{
         StyleBox["T", "TI"], "\[LongEqual]", "1.5", "\[Cross]", 
         SuperscriptBox["10", "7"]}], TraditionalForm], "errors" -> {}, 
     "input" -> "$T = 1.5 \\times 10^7$", "state" -> "Boxes"|>,
    "TeXAssistantTemplate"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "f880fc6d-164c-4387-b616-9f566221f0e4"],
 "s, the number of data samples is ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   TemplateBox[<|"boxes" -> FormBox[
       RowBox[{
         SubscriptBox[
          StyleBox["N", "TI"], "s"], "\[LongEqual]", "6", "\[Cross]", 
         SuperscriptBox["10", "10"]}], TraditionalForm], "errors" -> {}, 
     "input" -> "$N_{\\text{s}} = 6\\times10^{10}$", "state" -> "Boxes"|>,
    "TeXAssistantTemplate"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "d5233690-03e6-40a6-823c-78f28fbdbb30"],
 ", and the frequency resolution of the observation is \n",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   TemplateBox[<|"boxes" -> FormBox[
       Cell[
        BoxData[
         FormBox[
          GridBox[{{
             Cell[
              TextData[{" ", 
                Cell[
                 BoxData[
                  RowBox[{"\[CapitalDelta]", 
                    StyleBox["f", "TI"]}]], "InlineFormula"], " "}], CellTags -> 
              "eqn:SchutzEq12.43"], 
             Cell[
              TextData[{" ", 
                Cell[
                 BoxData[
                  RowBox[{"\[LongEqual]", "2", "/", 
                    StyleBox["T", "TI"], "\[LongEqual]", "1.3", "\[Cross]", 
                    SuperscriptBox["10", "-7"], "Hz", "."}]], 
                 "InlineFormula"], " "}]], 
             Cell[
              TextData[{" ", 
                Cell["Schutz Eq.\[NonBreakingSpace](12.43)", "InlineFormula"],
                 " "}]]}}], TraditionalForm]]], TraditionalForm], 
     "errors" -> {}, "input" -> 
     "\\begin{align}\\label{eqn:SchutzEq12.43}\n\\Delta f &= 2/T = \
1.3\\times10^{-7} \\text{Hz}.& \\mbox{Schutz Eq.~(12.43)}\n\\end{align}", 
     "state" -> "Boxes"|>,
    "TeXAssistantTemplate"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "c422c113-e912-4748-b542-d26fc0598ce0"]
}], "Subsubsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.963119059818204*^9, 3.963119064051423*^9}, 
   3.963119121814048*^9, {3.9631191982569427`*^9, 
   3.963119214784828*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"c13d306a-90bf-4130-938c-\
0fc46a5780b7"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"(*", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"Part", " ", 
     RowBox[{"(", "a", ")"}]}], ":", " ", 
    RowBox[{"Number", " ", "of", " ", "Data", " ", "Samples"}]}], "*)"}], 
  "\n", "\n", 
  RowBox[{"(*", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"The", " ", "problem", " ", "asks", " ", "for", " ", "Ns"}], ",", 
    " ", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{
     "the", " ", "number", " ", "of", " ", "samples", " ", "in", " ", "a", " ",
       "data", " ", "stream", " ", "of", " ", "\n", "duration", " ", "T"}], 
     " ", "=", " ", 
     RowBox[{"0.15", "e8", " ", "seconds", " ", 
      RowBox[{"(", 
       RowBox[{"about", " ", "one", " ", "half", " ", "year"}], ")"}], " ", 
      "with", " ", "a", " ", "sampling", " ", "rate", " ", "of", " ", "\n", 
      "4000", " ", 
      RowBox[{"Hz", "."}]}]}]}], "*)"}], "\n", "\n", 
  RowBox[{"(*", " ", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{
    "Duration", " ", "of", " ", "the", " ", "observation", " ", "in", " ", 
     "seconds"}], ",", " ", "Tobs"}], " ", "*)"}], "\n", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"Tobs", " ", "=", " ", 
     RowBox[{"0.15", "*", 
      RowBox[{"10", "^", "8"}]}]}], ";"}], "\n", 
   RowBox[{"(*", " ", 
    RowBox[{"Sampling", " ", "rate", " ", "in", " ", "Hz"}], " ", "*)"}], "\n", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"samplingRate", " ", "=", " ", "4000"}], ";"}], "\n", 
   RowBox[{"(*", " ", 
    RowBox[{"Number", " ", "of", " ", "samples", " ", "Ns"}], " ", "*)"}], "\n", 
   RowBox[{"Ns", " ", "=", " ", 
    RowBox[{"Tobs", " ", "*", " ", "samplingRate"}]}], "\n"}]}]], "Code",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.963119342298307*^9, 3.963119379207122*^9}, {
   3.96316846726686*^9, 3.963168506605505*^9}, 3.9631685367527742`*^9, {
   3.963169106946971*^9, 
   3.963169108112131*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"9a4a8750-9fbc-4f46-be5b-\
8c43e50f3a73"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"(*", " ", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"For", " ", "the", " ", "sake", " ", "of", " ", "curiousity"}], ",",
     " ", 
    RowBox[{
    "here", " ", "is", " ", "a", " ", "much", " ", "more", " ", "precise", " ",
      "calculation"}]}], " ", "*)"}], "\n", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"secperyear", " ", "=", " ", 
     RowBox[{"365.2425", " ", "*", " ", "24", " ", "*", " ", "3600"}]}], 
    ";"}], "\n", 
   RowBox[{"Nsprecise", " ", "=", " ", 
    RowBox[{"0.4*^4", " ", "*", " ", "secperyear", " ", "*", " ", 
     RowBox[{"(", 
      RowBox[{"1", "/", "2"}], ")"}]}]}], "\n"}]}]], "Code",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.963168631616858*^9, {3.963168678599388*^9, 3.963168739103099*^9}},
 CellLabel->
  "In[106]:=",ExpressionUUID->"876d3c2a-c822-4582-aa02-c8de4ee8195c"],

Cell["\<\
The frequency resolution is calculated from the reciprocal of the longest \
period that is resolved, which here is 6 months. Schutz is more conservative \
and  assumes half this resolution.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.963168868444003*^9, 3.96316893396428*^9}, {
  3.9631690739171124`*^9, 
  3.96316908972957*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"315e05f3-b7f9-46be-aef4-\
21590c3467fb"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"(*", " ", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"Frequency", " ", "resolution"}], ",", " ", 
    RowBox[{"Delta", " ", "f", " ", "in", " ", "Hz"}]}], "*)"}], "\n", 
  RowBox[{"Deltaf", " ", "=", " ", 
   RowBox[{"1", "/", "Tobs"}]}]}]], "Code",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9631689512639236`*^9, 3.9631690353672028`*^9}},
 CellLabel->
  "In[108]:=",ExpressionUUID->"4f8996b8-9a8f-4487-b10f-7f43401b467b"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[TextData[{
 "(b) ... Assuming that the interpolation requires of order ten operations \
per data point, show that each demodulation requires ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   TemplateBox[<|"boxes" -> FormBox[
       RowBox[{
         SubscriptBox[
          StyleBox["N", "TI"], "demod"], "\[LongEqual]", "6", "\[Cross]", 
         SuperscriptBox["10", "11"]}], TraditionalForm], "errors" -> {}, 
     "input" -> "$N_{\\text{demod}} = 6\\times10^{11}$", "state" -> "Boxes"|>,
    
    "TeXAssistantTemplate"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "0adfd81c-a32f-47ea-a2f9-b46e5e2170ae"],
 " operations.There is, of course, one demodulation for each resolvable patch \
on the sky."
}], "Subsubsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.963169326210207*^9, 3.963169347195758*^9}, {
  3.963169398173184*^9, 3.963169431661446*^9}},
 TextJustification->1.,ExpressionUUID->"76b85376-78fd-4e22-b586-42d57e6a39fe"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"(*", " ", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{
    "Number", " ", "of", " ", "operations", " ", "to", " ", "demodulate", " ",
      "the", " ", "Doppler", " ", "shift", " ", "for", " ", "data", " ", 
     "from", " ", "a", " ", "given", " ", "patch", "\n", "of", " ", "the", " ",
      "sky"}], ",", " ", "Ndemod"}], " ", "*)"}], "\n", 
  RowBox[{"Ndemod", " ", "=", " ", 
   RowBox[{"10", "*", "Ns"}]}]}]], "Code",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.963169452378455*^9, {3.963169490267308*^9, 3.963169500095172*^9}, {
   3.963169562101591*^9, 
   3.963169635084424*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"70c067a6-b207-4ba0-8f81-\
07361a8e2e11"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[TextData[{
 "(c) To correct for the pulsar\[CloseCurlyQuote]s changing signal frequency, \
we will assume that the only parameter we need is the first time-derivative \
of the frequency, i.e. that the frequency changes linearly in time over the \
observation period. We shall handle this in the same way as the demodulation, \
by shifting to a different time measure. We assume that the source\
\[CloseCurlyQuote]s wavefronts are emitted at a uniform rate with respect to \
a clock that is slowing down. Once we map that clock\[CloseCurlyQuote]s time \
onto barycenter time, we restore the pulsar to a single-frequency signal and \
we can use a Fourier transform to search for it. Letting ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   TemplateBox[<|"boxes" -> FormBox[
       RowBox[{
         StyleBox["t", "TI"], "\[LongEqual]", "0"}], TraditionalForm], 
     "errors" -> {}, "input" -> "$t = 0$", "state" -> "Boxes"|>,
    "TeXAssistantTemplate"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "642a5a37-81cd-485c-a1e9-491b0621909b"],
 " represent the start of the observation time in the barycenter, we can give \
concrete expression to this by relating the pulsar\[CloseCurlyQuote]s `clock\
\[CloseCurlyQuote] ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   TemplateBox[<|"boxes" -> FormBox[
       StyleBox["T", "TI"], TraditionalForm], "errors" -> {}, "input" -> 
     "$T$", "state" -> "Boxes"|>,
    "TeXAssistantTemplate"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "bf456104-0696-4a37-a156-ff0073ad84bd"],
 " to barycentric time t as follows:\n",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   TemplateBox[<|"boxes" -> FormBox[
       Cell[
        BoxData[
         FormBox[
          GridBox[{{
             Cell[
              TextData[{" ", 
                Cell[
                 BoxData[
                  SubscriptBox[
                   StyleBox["t", "TI"], "p"]], "InlineFormula"], " "}], 
              CellTags -> "eqn:SchutzEq12.44"], 
             Cell[
              TextData[{" ", 
                Cell[
                 BoxData[
                  RowBox[{"\[LongEqual]", 
                    StyleBox["t", "TI"], 
                    RowBox[{"(", 
                    RowBox[{"1", "-", 
                    StyleBox["t", "TI"], "/", "\[Tau]"}], ")"}], ","}]], 
                 "InlineFormula"], " "}]], 
             Cell[
              TextData[{" ", 
                Cell["Schutz Eq.\[NonBreakingSpace](12.44)", "InlineFormula"],
                 " "}]]}}], TraditionalForm]]], TraditionalForm], 
     "errors" -> {}, "input" -> 
     "\\begin{align}\\label{eqn:SchutzEq12.44}\nt_{\\text{p}} &= t(1- \
t/\\tau), &\\mbox{Schutz Eq.~(12.44)}\n\\end{align}", "state" -> "Boxes"|>,
    "TeXAssistantTemplate"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "fa58f3be-7370-4da0-accf-89f5f7aedadc"],
 "\nwhere ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   TemplateBox[<|
    "boxes" -> FormBox["\[Tau]", TraditionalForm], "errors" -> {}, "input" -> 
     "$\\tau$", "state" -> "Boxes"|>,
    "TeXAssistantTemplate"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "03fa60bf-f196-4eef-a80c-06bd7ab363ef"],
 " is a parameter known as the slowdown time, a measure of how long it takes \
the pulsar to change its frequency by a significant amount. Since realistic \
slowdown times are larger than ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   TemplateBox[<|"boxes" -> FormBox[
       SuperscriptBox["10", "3"], TraditionalForm], "errors" -> {}, "input" -> 
     "$10^3$", "state" -> "Boxes"|>,
    "TeXAssistantTemplate"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "7d555f4d-6d8d-4da4-8e04-b8f9969b4f0b"],
 " years, we always have ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   TemplateBox[<|"boxes" -> FormBox[
       RowBox[{
         StyleBox["t", "TI"], "\[LessEqual]", 
         StyleBox["T", "TI"], "\[LessLess]", "\[Tau]"}], TraditionalForm], 
     "errors" -> {}, "input" -> "$t \\le T \\ll \\tau$", "state" -> "Boxes"|>,
    
    "TeXAssistantTemplate"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "13e75b33-799f-41fb-a1c5-8bd5f477aabe"],
 ", so that the fractional changes in clock rates are small.\n"
}], "Subsubsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.963170296136177*^9, 3.963170315306682*^9}, {
  3.963170393452075*^9, 3.963170600852749*^9}},
 TextJustification->1.,ExpressionUUID->"048be2b7-7f6f-46b5-b8b0-72890ab842b9"],

